PNT.registerModule({"id": "m01", "number": 1, "title": "PNT Fundamentals", "subtitle": "", "version": "0.1", "objectives": ["Explain what Positioning, Navigation and Timing mean as distinct capabilities.", "Distinguish PNT, GNSS and GPS correctly.", "Describe a simple end-to-end PNT chain from source to application.", "Recognise that a PNT capability may depend on upstream infrastructure, services and interfaces that are not visible to the consuming application.", "Explain why timing is fundamental both to GNSS positioning and to non-navigation systems.", "Distinguish accuracy, precision, integrity, availability and continuity.", "Identify different types of PNT dependency in an operational system.", "Explain why multiple GNSS constellations do not automatically create resilient PNT.", "Ask useful first-order engineering questions about a PNT-dependent system."], "lessons": [{"id": "welcome-to-pnt-engineering", "title": "Welcome to PNT Engineering", "html": "<p>Positioning, Navigation and Timing (PNT) capabilities underpin far more of modern life than satellite navigation. They support transport, communications, financial systems, digital infrastructure, emergency services, defence systems and Critical National Infrastructure. In many systems, PNT is an enabling dependency that is largely invisible until it becomes degraded, unavailable or misleading.</p>\n<p>This learning programme is designed to give Daintta employees a structured engineering understanding of PNT. It begins with the fundamental concepts and terminology, then progressively develops knowledge of GNSS, alternative PNT technologies, threats and vulnerabilities, resilient architectures, systems engineering, verification and assurance.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The central idea</p><div class=\"callout-body\">PNT is not the same thing as GPS, and it is not the same thing as GNSS. PNT describes capabilities. GNSS is one family of technologies used to provide those capabilities.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m01-how-this-module-works\">How this module works</h2>\n<p>Each module follows the same learning pattern: understand what you are going to learn; work through the core teaching; apply the concepts to engineering examples; test your understanding; then review the key takeaways and authoritative external resources for deeper study.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m01-learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<p>By the end of Module 1, you should be able to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Explain what Positioning, Navigation and Timing mean as distinct capabilities.</p></li>\n<li><p>Distinguish PNT, GNSS and GPS correctly.</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe a simple end-to-end PNT chain from source to application.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recognise that a PNT capability may depend on upstream infrastructure, services and interfaces that are not visible to the consuming application.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain why timing is fundamental both to GNSS positioning and to non-navigation systems.</p></li>\n<li><p>Distinguish accuracy, precision, integrity, availability and continuity.</p></li>\n<li><p>Identify different types of PNT dependency in an operational system.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain why multiple GNSS constellations do not automatically create resilient PNT.</p></li>\n<li><p>Ask useful first-order engineering questions about a PNT-dependent system.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m01-where-this-fits-in-the-course\">Where this fits in the course</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Stage</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Modules</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Focus</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Foundation</td>\n<td>1–4</td>\n<td>PNT concepts, GNSS and performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Technology &amp; resilience</td>\n<td>5–7</td>\n<td>Alternative PNT, threats and resilient architectures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Engineering application</td>\n<td>8–10</td>\n<td>Systems engineering, assurance and applied scenarios</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "what-is-pnt", "title": "What is PNT?", "html": "<p>PNT stands for Positioning, Navigation and Timing. Although these capabilities are closely related, they solve different problems and can have very different engineering requirements.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m01-positioning\">2.1 Positioning</h2>\n<p>Positioning answers the question: Where am I, or where is something?</p>\n<p>A position is determined relative to a reference system. It may be expressed as latitude, longitude and altitude, but positioning is not limited to geographic coordinates. A system might determine a three-dimensional position, an object's location relative to another object, a location inside a building, or a position underwater.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Engineering point</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Do not assume that a requirement for 'position' means latitude and longitude. Establish the reference frame, dimensions, accuracy, update rate and operating environment required by the application.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m01-navigation\">2.2 Navigation</h2>\n<p>Navigation answers: Where am I going, and how do I get there?</p>\n<p>Navigation uses position together with information such as destination, velocity, heading, route constraints and the surrounding environment to determine movement or a path. A GNSS receiver that outputs coordinates is providing position information. A vessel that combines GNSS, inertial sensors, heading, charts and route information to decide where to steer is performing navigation.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: What is PNT?\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m01-image1.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m01-timing\">2.3 Timing</h2>\n<p>Timing answers: What time is it, and how accurately must systems agree on that time?</p>\n<p>Accurate and precise time is essential to many systems that never physically navigate. Telecommunications networks, electricity infrastructure, distributed computing and financial systems can depend on synchronised time. This means an organisation can be critically PNT-dependent even when no vehicle, person or platform is navigating.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Think beyond navigation</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A PNT assessment that only examines maps, vehicles and GNSS receivers can miss critical timing dependencies.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m01-knowledge-pause\">Knowledge pause</h2>\n<p>Imagine a mobile telecommunications base station. Its physical position is fixed. It may have little or no operational navigation requirement, yet accurate timing and synchronisation can be essential. This is still a PNT dependency.</p>"}, {"id": "pnt-is-a-capability-not-a-single-technology", "title": "PNT is a Capability, not a Single Technology", "html": "<p>One of the most important habits in PNT engineering is separating the required capability from the technology used to provide it. GPS is a technology. GNSS is a category of satellite navigation systems. PNT is the capability the application consumes.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: PNT is a Capability, not a Single Technology\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m01-image2.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m01-the-pnt-chain\">3.1 The PNT chain</h2>\n<p>A useful first-order mental model is:</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">SOURCE → SIGNAL / MEASUREMENT → RECEIVER / SENSOR → PNT SOLUTION → APPLICATION</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>This model helps engineers identify where dependencies and failure modes exist. An application may receive a position from a navigation computer without knowing that the navigation computer ultimately depends on a GNSS antenna, an RF front end, satellite signals and external timing.</p>\n<p>It also helps separate the loss of a source from the loss of the final capability. A system may temporarily maintain a useful navigation or timing solution after its primary source is lost, for example through inertial navigation or clock holdover. We will examine these mechanisms later in the course.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m01-the-pnt-dependency-chain\">3.2 The PNT Dependency Chain</h2>\n<p>The simple PNT chain also reveals something important about real systems: the application may depend on infrastructure and services that are several steps removed from the PNT information it consumes.</p>\n<p>For example, an application receiving an accurate position may depend upon:</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Satellite or Terrestrial Source → Signal → Antenna → Receiver → Correction Service → Communications Network → Navigation Processor → Application</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>A timing-dependent system may similarly rely on:</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">External Time Reference → Receiver → Local Clock → Timing Distribution Network → Consuming Systems</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>This wider sequence can be thought of as the <strong>PNT dependency chain</strong>. In some contexts it is also described as part of the PNT value chain because PNT information is generated, distributed, transformed and consumed across multiple systems and organisations.</p>\n<p>The important engineering point is that failure can occur anywhere along this chain. A GNSS receiver may be healthy while its antenna has failed. A high-accuracy receiver may continue operating while its correction service is unavailable. A telecommunications system may receive time through a network whose upstream reference ultimately depends on GNSS.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Engineering Principle</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Do not stop dependency analysis at the component that provides PNT directly to the application. Trace the capability upstream until you understand the sources, infrastructure, interfaces and services on which it ultimately depends.</div></aside>\n<p>Later modules will extend this idea into common-mode failure, resilient architectures, system boundaries and system-of-systems dependency analysis.</p>"}, {"id": "gps-gnss-and-constellations", "title": "GPS, GNSS and Constellations", "html": "<p>GNSS means Global Navigation Satellite System. It is the generic term for satellite constellations that provide positioning, navigation and timing services. GPS is the United States system and is one GNSS, not a synonym for all GNSS.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Constellation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Operator</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GPS</td>\n<td>United States</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Galileo</td>\n<td>European Union</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GLONASS</td>\n<td>Russia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>BeiDou</td>\n<td>China</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"m01-multi-constellation-versus-multi-frequency\">4.1 Multi-constellation versus multi-frequency</h2>\n<p>These terms describe different forms of capability. Multi-constellation means a receiver can use more than one GNSS constellation. Multi-frequency means it can use more than one radio frequency or signal band. A receiver can be one, the other, both, or neither.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Remember</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Multi-constellation = multiple satellite systems. Multi-frequency = multiple signal frequencies. Neither term, by itself, proves that the overall PNT solution is resilient.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "why-timing-matters-so-much", "title": "Why Timing Matters So Much", "html": "<p>Satellite positioning is fundamentally a ranging problem based on very precise time measurements. A GNSS satellite broadcasts information describing where it is and when its signal was transmitted. The receiver compares transmission and reception timing to estimate the signal travel time.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Approximate range = signal travel time × speed of light</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>Radio signals travel at approximately 300,000 kilometres per second. A timing error of one microsecond therefore corresponds to roughly 300 metres of signal travel distance; one nanosecond corresponds to roughly 30 centimetres. This is why clocks, synchronisation and propagation delays are central engineering concerns in PNT.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m01-why-four-satellites\">5.1 Why four satellites?</h2>\n<p>At a conceptual level, a GNSS receiver is solving for four unknowns: three spatial coordinates (X, Y and Z) plus receiver clock bias. Consumer receivers do not carry satellite-grade atomic clocks, so the clock error must be estimated as part of the navigation solution. This is why a conventional three-dimensional GNSS fix normally requires measurements from at least four satellites.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Why Timing Matters So Much\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m01-image3.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "pnt-performance-accuracy-is-not-enough", "title": "PNT Performance: Accuracy is not Enough", "html": "<p>A claim such as ‘our system is accurate to 50 cm’ is incomplete. PNT performance is multi-dimensional. Engineers need to understand what performance is required, how it is measured and what happens when the system cannot meet it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Characteristic</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Practical question</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Accuracy</td>\n<td>How close the reported value is to the true value.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Precision</td>\n<td>How repeatable or tightly grouped repeated measurements are. A result can be precise but consistently wrong.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Availability</td>\n<td>Whether the required PNT service is available when it is needed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Continuity</td>\n<td>Whether the service can continue without an unexpected interruption during the required operation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Integrity</td>\n<td>The degree of trust that can be placed in the information, including the ability to identify and warn when it should not be used.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: PNT Performance: Accuracy is not Enough\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m01-image4.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Why integrity matters</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A system that reports no usable position may be operationally disruptive. A system that continues to report a convincing but incorrect position can be more dangerous because downstream systems or operators may continue to act on bad information.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "understanding-pnt-dependency", "title": "Understanding PNT Dependency", "html": "<p>Before judging whether a system is resilient, establish what PNT capability it actually consumes and why. Different applications can have radically different requirements.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Example</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Position</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Navigation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Illustrative concern</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Surveying</td>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Secondary</td>\n<td>Centimetre-level position may be required.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Telecommunications</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>Very high</td>\n<td>Loss of synchronisation can affect service.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Autonomous maritime platform</td>\n<td>Critical</td>\n<td>Critical</td>\n<td>Important</td>\n<td>Misleading navigation data may create operational or safety risk.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Defence platform</td>\n<td>Mission-dependent</td>\n<td>Mission-dependent</td>\n<td>Mission-dependent</td>\n<td>Threat environment and ability to operate when GNSS is denied may dominate the design.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Useful dependency questions include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Which PNT functions does the system depend upon: position, navigation, timing, or a combination?</p></li>\n<li><p>What performance is required for each function?</p></li>\n<li><p>What operational task depends on that performance?</p></li>\n<li><p>What happens if PNT becomes unavailable?</p></li>\n<li><p>What happens if PNT remains available but becomes misleading?</p></li>\n<li><p>How quickly must degradation or erroneous information be detected?</p></li>\n<li><p>How long can the system continue in a degraded state?</p></li>\n<li><p>What evidence demonstrates that the claimed performance and resilience are achieved?</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "the-first-resilience-lesson-diversity-is-not-independence", "title": "The First Resilience Lesson: Diversity is not Independence", "html": "<p>Consider a receiver that uses GPS, Galileo and BeiDou. This provides useful constellation diversity and can improve satellite visibility and performance. It does not, however, automatically mean that the PNT capability is resilient.</p>\n<p>All of those constellations are space-based systems whose signals must reach the user through radio-frequency paths and be received by GNSS equipment. A sufficiently severe local interference event may affect several constellations at once. The receiver may also have common antenna, cabling, power, processing or integration dependencies.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: The First Resilience Lesson: Diversity is not Independence\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m01-image5.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Engineering principle</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Redundancy, diversity and dissimilarity are related but are not synonyms. A resilient design considers whether alternative sources have genuinely different failure modes. We will develop this in Module 7.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "speaking-like-a-pnt-engineer", "title": "Speaking Like a PNT Engineer", "html": "<p>Good PNT conversations start with requirements, dependencies and evidence rather than product names. If a supplier says, 'our solution is resilient to spoofing', an engineering response is not simply to accept the feature claim.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">A stronger assurance question</p><div class=\"callout-body\">What evidence demonstrates that claim, against which threat model, under what operating conditions, and what does the system do when the threat is detected?</div></aside>\n<p>As the course progresses, you will learn enough about PNT technologies and failure modes to make those questions increasingly specific.</p>"}, {"id": "module-summary", "title": "Module Summary", "html": "<p>You should now understand the following core ideas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>PNT describes Positioning, Navigation and Timing capabilities; it is broader than satellite navigation.</p></li>\n<li><p>GPS is one GNSS constellation. GNSS is one family of technologies capable of providing PNT.</p></li>\n<li><p>Position, navigation and timing can have different operational importance in the same system.</p></li>\n<li><p>A useful engineering model is Source → Signal/Measurement → Receiver/Sensor → PNT Solution → Application.</p></li>\n<li><p>GNSS ranging depends on precise time measurement, and receiver clock bias is one of the unknowns solved by the receiver.</p></li>\n<li><p>Accuracy alone is not enough. Precision, availability, continuity and especially integrity also matter.</p></li>\n<li><p>A system can be PNT-dependent even if nothing moves; timing is a major example.</p></li>\n<li><p>Multiple GNSS constellations do not automatically provide resilient PNT because common-mode dependencies can remain.</p></li>\n<li><p>PNT engineering conversations should focus on requirements, failure consequences, threat conditions and evidence.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "key-terms", "title": "Key Terms", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Working definition</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT</td>\n<td>Positioning, Navigation and Timing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>GNSS</td>\n<td>Global Navigation Satellite System, the generic category of satellite navigation constellations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GPS</td>\n<td>The United States Global Positioning System, one GNSS constellation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Positioning</td>\n<td>Determining location and orientation relative to a reference.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Navigation</td>\n<td>Determining current and desired position and how to move between them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Timing</td>\n<td>Acquiring and maintaining accurate and precise time from a reference.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Accuracy</td>\n<td>Closeness of a reported value to truth.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Precision</td>\n<td>Repeatability or closeness of repeated measurements to each other.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Availability</td>\n<td>Whether the required service is available when needed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Continuity</td>\n<td>Ability of the service to continue without unexpected interruption during an operation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Integrity</td>\n<td>Trust in the information and the ability to identify/warn when it should not be used.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Common-mode failure</td>\n<td>A failure mechanism capable of affecting multiple nominally redundant elements at the same time.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "further-learning", "title": "Further Learning", "html": "<p>The following open-source resources provide authoritative background and are recommended for learners who want to explore the subject beyond this module.</p>\n<p><strong>UK Government: Positioning, Navigation and Timing Overview</strong><br/>\nNational PNT Office / DSIT overview of PNT, UK dependency, resilience and current policy.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/guidance/positioning-navigation-and-timing-overview\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/guidance/positioning-navigation-and-timing-overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK Government: Positioning, Navigation and Timing Collection</strong><br/>\nCentral collection of UK PNT guidance, research and announcements.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/positioning-navigation-and-timing\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/positioning-navigation-and-timing</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK Government: Space-based PNT Technical Concepts</strong><br/>\nTechnical concepts developed under the UK Space Based PNT Programme.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/space-based-pnt-technical-concepts\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/space-based-pnt-technical-concepts</a></p>\n<p><strong>GPS.gov: GPS Performance</strong><br/>\nBackground on GPS performance characteristics including accuracy, integrity, continuity and availability.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gps.gov/gps-performance\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gps.gov/gps-performance</a></p>\n<p><strong>UK Space Agency: Space PNT</strong><br/>\nCurrent UK Space Agency material covering space-based PNT programmes and development.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/space-pnt\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/space-pnt</a></p>\n<p><strong>Royal Institute of Navigation: Introduction to PNT in the 21st Century</strong></p>\n<p>Structured professional learning covering the foundations of Positioning, Navigation and Timing, GNSS, threats, resilient PNT, alternative technologies, emerging PNT and time and frequency distribution. Useful complementary learning for those wanting to extend the foundation provided by this programme.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.pathlms.com/rin/courses/120419\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.pathlms.com/rin/courses/120419</a></p>"}, {"id": "next-module", "title": "Next Module", "html": "<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">MODULE 02 — HOW GNSS WORKS</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>Module 2 moves from the PNT capability model into the engineering of satellite navigation. It will cover satellite constellations and orbits, ranging and pseudoranges, satellite and receiver clocks, navigation messages, ephemeris and almanac data, coordinate concepts, signal acquisition and the basic process by which a receiver produces a PVT (position, velocity and time) solution.</p>"}], "practice": [{"n": 1, "prompt": "Which statement best describes PNT?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Another name for GPS."}, {"id": "B", "text": "A set of positioning, navigation and timing capabilities that can be provided by multiple technologies."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The collective name for GPS, Galileo, GLONASS and BeiDou only."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A radio frequency used by satellite navigation."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "PNT describes capabilities, not a single technology."}, {"n": 2, "prompt": "Which are GNSS constellations?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "GPS"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Galileo"}, {"id": "C", "text": "BeiDou"}, {"id": "D", "text": "INS"}, {"id": "E", "text": "GLONASS"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "E"], "rationale": "GPS, Galileo, BeiDou and GLONASS are GNSS constellations. INS is an inertial navigation technology."}, {"n": 3, "prompt": "Why does a conventional 3D GNSS solution normally require at least four satellite measurements?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "One satellite is required for each compass direction."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Three determine position and the fourth provides internet connectivity."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The receiver solves for X, Y, Z and receiver clock bias."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Four satellites are required to overcome the curvature of the Earth."}], "answer": ["C"], "rationale": "The receiver estimates three spatial coordinates plus its own clock bias."}, {"n": 4, "prompt": "Which statement correctly distinguishes accuracy from integrity?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Accuracy is closeness to truth; integrity concerns whether the information can be trusted and unsafe information identified in time."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Accuracy and integrity mean the same thing."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Integrity is repeatability; accuracy is availability."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Accuracy applies only to position while integrity applies only to timing."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "A solution may be accurate most of the time yet still require mechanisms to detect when it should not be trusted."}, {"n": 5, "prompt": "Why might GPS + Galileo + BeiDou still share common-mode vulnerabilities?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "They all rely on space-based RF signals reaching the user."}, {"id": "B", "text": "They may share the same receiving antenna and RF chain."}, {"id": "C", "text": "A local interference source may affect more than one constellation."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Using three constellations guarantees completely independent failure modes."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C"], "rationale": "Constellation diversity does not remove shared RF, antenna, receiver or local interference dependencies."}, {"n": 6, "prompt": "Which example best illustrates a critical timing dependency with little navigation dependency?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "A fixed telecommunications base station requiring network synchronisation."}, {"id": "B", "text": "A paper road atlas."}, {"id": "C", "text": "A mechanical compass."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A hiking trail marker."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "Telecommunications systems can depend heavily on precise synchronisation without navigating."}, {"n": 7, "prompt": "Which situation can be particularly hazardous from an integrity perspective?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The system clearly reports that no PNT solution is available."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The system provides plausible but incorrect PNT information without timely warning."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The system is powered off for maintenance."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A map is stored locally."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Undetected misleading information can continue to drive incorrect downstream decisions."}, {"n": 8, "prompt": "Which questions are useful when assessing a PNT-dependent system?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "What PNT functions does the application require?"}, {"id": "B", "text": "What happens if the information is unavailable or misleading?"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Are alternative sources susceptible to common-mode failure?"}, {"id": "D", "text": "What evidence supports the claimed performance and resilience?"}, {"id": "E", "text": "Does the supplier use the word 'resilient' in its marketing material?"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "rationale": "Engineering assessment focuses on required capability, consequences, failure modes and evidence."}]});
